<section class="section soft"><div class="wrap"><div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">For international property owners</div><h2>Your issue may not be “foreigners law” at all</h2></div><p>A foreign owner whose building enters urban transformation has an urban transformation problem. A foreign investor affected by a zoning-plan change has a zoning problem. A buyer facing an undisclosed mortgage has a real-estate problem. These matters are handled through the relevant Turkish property, planning and construction law framework.</p></div><div class="article-grid"><div class="article-card"><div class="tag">Property Owner</div><h3>“My apartment in Turkey is being demolished and rebuilt.”</h3><p>The relevant questions may concern urban transformation, ownership, contractor terms, project documents and the owner’s rights during redevelopment.</p></div><div class="article-card"><div class="tag">Land Owner</div><h3>“The zoning plan affecting my land has changed.”</h3><p>The issue may involve planning status, development rights, administrative decisions and the legal consequences of a plan amendment.</p></div><div class="article-card"><div class="tag">Buyer</div><h3>“I want to know what I am actually buying.”</h3><p>The work may require title deed due diligence, permit review, contract analysis and verification of the property before payment.</p></div></div></div></section>

